Linux kernel, https://git.kernel.org (GPL-2.0)
  Every release ships a kernel: techo5-dot-kernel-bt.zImage-dtb, Linux 3.18.19 for 32-bit ARM. It is
  GPL-2.0, and this is where its source is. Releases after dot-v1.0.18 also ship the Echo 2nd gen's,
  techo5-dot-kernel-bt-radar.zImage-dtb, built the same way; where it differs is noted below.
    - Base: Amazon's GPL source release for the Echo Dot (2nd Generation),
      Echo_Dot_src-6.5.7.1-20251024, kernel/mediatek/mt8163/3.18_hl inside platform.tar. Amazon
      publish their source releases at
      https://www.amazon.com/gp/help/customer/display.html?nodeId=G202096090 (docs/hardware.md).
      The Echo 2nd gen's: Amazon's GPL source release for the Echo (2nd Generation),
      Echo_v2_src-6.5.7.1-20251024, the same path inside its platform.tar.
    - Bluetooth: Amazon's tarball carries only the files their build compiles, so net/bluetooth,
      drivers/bluetooth and include/net/bluetooth are taken from upstream linux-3.18.19, and only
      where Amazon's tree has no file of its own.
    - Changes: the 48 patches in tools/linux/kernel-patches, applied in order, each naming its
      upstream commit, the stable backport it came from, its CVE and any 3.18 adaptation.
    - Configuration: built from the running kernel's own config, recorded as
      docs/dumps/biscuit-fireos6-echolocal-boot-3.18.19.config (the Echo 2nd gen's:
      docs/dumps/radar-fireos6-boot-3.18.19.config).
    - The script that does all of it is tools/linux/build-kernel.sh, and docs/building.md is how to
      run it. A rebuild uses the same source, configuration and patches as the shipped image. It is
      not guaranteed to come out byte for byte the same: the build time and the build directory are
      compiled in.
  Boot images, which wrap that kernel with a unit's own ramdisk, are built per unit and not
  distributed.

  Releases from dot-v1.0.31 also ship Bluetooth kernel modules for the Echo Dot 3rd gen
  (bluetooth.ko, hci_vhci.ko and cmac.ko, Linux 4.4.22 for 64-bit ARM, in
  techo5-donut-rootfs-<version>.tar.gz under /usr/lib/techo5/modules). They are GPL-2.0, and each
  release carries their complete source: techo5-donut-bt-modules-source.tar.xz.
    - Base: Amazon's GPL source release for the Echo Input, Echo_Input_src-6.5.4.7-20210719, as
      imported in https://github.com/bengris32/android_kernel_amazon_cupcake (commit d16134f). Amazon
      has not published the Echo Dot 3rd gen's; the Echo Input's is the same 4.4.22 MT8516 tree.
    - Bluetooth: net/bluetooth, drivers/bluetooth, include/net/bluetooth, crypto/cmac.c and two build
      files are taken unchanged from upstream linux-4.4.22, only where Amazon's tree has no file of its
      own (tools/linux/bt-modules/added-from-4.4.22.txt). Nothing else is changed.
    - Configuration: the tree's cupcake_defconfig with Bluetooth, the virtual HCI driver and CMAC
      turned on as modules.
    - The script that does all of it is tools/linux/build-bt-modules.sh. It builds in a fixed
      directory, so no path of the machine that built them is compiled in.
  The Echo Dot 3rd gen's kernel itself is the unit's own, from its own boot partition, and is never
  distributed.

wpa_supplicant, https://w1.fi/wpa_supplicant/
  Copyright (c) Jouni Malinen and contributors, BSD license. The Echo Dot 3rd gen's root filesystem
  carries Alpine's 2.11 rebuilt with tools/linux/wpa-patches/0100-donut-no-wmm-replay-counters.patch
  (tools/linux/build-wpa-supplicant.sh).

Shairport Sync, nqptp and librespot
  Both boards' root filesystems carry three programs built by TECHO5's tools/linux scripts, with their
  license files in /usr/share/licenses and in tools/linux/licenses here:
    - Shairport Sync 5.6.1, https://github.com/mikebrady/shairport-sync, the AirPlay 2 receiver, as
      /usr/local/bin/techo5-shairport-sync. MIT, with parts under BSD and ISC licenses (its LICENSES
      file). Copyright (c) 2014-2026 Mike Brady and the other authors it names. Unmodified; FFmpeg's
      ALAC and AAC decoders and swresample (LGPL-2.1-or-later, https://ffmpeg.org) are linked in
      statically, and TECHO5's tools/linux/build-shairport-sync.sh rebuilds or relinks it.
    - nqptp 1.2.9, https://github.com/mikebrady/nqptp, the clock AirPlay 2 needs, as
      /usr/local/bin/techo5-nqptp. GPL-2.0. Changed by TECHO5's tools/linux/nqptp-run-as-user.patch
      (adds -u <user>), which is GPL-2.0 too.
    - librespot 0.8.0, https://github.com/librespot-org/librespot, the Spotify Connect receiver, as
      /usr/local/bin/techo5-librespot. MIT, Copyright (c) 2015 Paul Lietar. Changed by TECHO5's
      tools/linux/librespot-volume-fifo.patch (adds --volume-fifo); its Rust dependencies, named by its
      Cargo.lock, keep their own licenses (most MIT or Apache-2.0).
  Their source archives, with the patches, licenses and build scripts, are attached to each release
  from dot-v1.0.35 on (techo5-third-party-sources*.tar.gz).

Alpine Linux, https://alpinelinux.org
  The release root filesystem is assembled from Alpine 3.24 armv7 packages, unmodified except
  bluealsa; each package keeps its own license, and its source is published by Alpine.

docs/dumps/*.config are kernel configurations generated from Amazon's GPL 3.18.19 kernel for
biscuit and radar, as embedded in the published boot images.
